#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483} :activity/id #uuid "3a675a3e-a62b-5b11-9c31-522dea85c72d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/id #uuid "6cd92831-a889-52a8-bcc8-0868b82263c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125483 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125815 :tutorial/content "Suppose we know that the gradient of a function $f(x,y)$ is \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2xy-3x^2) \\ \\mathbf i + (x^2-2y) \\ \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\n \n\nHow can we reconstruct the function $f(x,y)$ knowing only its gradient?\n\nSince the components of $\\nabla f$ must be the first-order partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y$ respectively, we know that \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x} =  2xy-3x^2,   \\qquad \\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}=x^2-2y.}\n$$\n\nIntegrating  $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ as a constant, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\int \\left(2xy-3x^2\\right) \\textrm d  x = x^2y -x^3 +g(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(y)$ is an arbitrary function of $y.$ Intuitively, $g(y)$ contains all the terms that would vanish when differentiated with respect to $x.$\n\nNow, differentiating the above with respect to $y$ gives \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y} = x^2 + g'(y).\n$$\n\nSo, equating the two expressions for $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{ \nx^2 + g'(y) &= x^2-2y\\ \n        g'(y)  & = -2y.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle g(y)=\\int(-2y) \\ \\textrm d y =  -y^2 +C.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x^2y -x^3-y^2 +C .\n$$\n\nNote that the above procedure is symmetric with respect to $x$ and $y.$ We could have started by integrating $\\dfrac {\\partial f}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y$ and then differentiating with respect to $x.$ The final result would have been the same." :tutorial/id #uuid "3d2fa632-58e8-5d68-a8e2-fb8822d211c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4256 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "063b4918-369c-5e01-892c-07d82af9b650" :step/math-academy-id 25881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125484 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124569 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124378 :question/id #uuid "ff1853b3-627e-5c73-be98-b1d9a586f198" :question/math-academy-id "e-7020"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97730a2e-3d3e-5a2c-b366-7a3b1eafbe63" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050983}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125061} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235530} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266130} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266131} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351362} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406697} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406699} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406700} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Two-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "c64d2d4c-15fd-55c0-90c6-c2215440ba8d" :step/math-academy-id 25882 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125494 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124574 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124382 :question/id #uuid "09d12c46-4ac8-53d9-80a6-ca68f98e0c5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "90458c6e-3d72-5235-a1c0-627fe4fd2855" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323869} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351365} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406698}] :knowledge-point/title "Reconstructing a Three-Variable Function From Its Gradient"} :step/id #uuid "65be1eef-6324-59ea-bbe6-d93f3c5b9154" :step/math-academy-id 26444}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124740}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421429} :activity/id #uuid "1a5e1aad-99c7-56df-9701-94352de75a5a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050869 :topic/id #uuid "6cd92831-a889-52a8-bcc8-0868b82263c0" :topic/math-academy-id 1951} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61fd2894-92e2-5f21-a8ef-3e4ce0f75c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+{x}^{2}-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263843} {:answer/id #uuid "79e27da1-668f-5a0d-929e-edada25fd557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264058} {:answer/id #uuid "7c4936ba-0c0b-5085-a53f-9463a50276eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy+x{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264073} {:answer/id #uuid "ab37d3c3-0675-5158-a1cc-82765ce5104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} {:answer/id #uuid "f52f7f29-86a2-537c-90f4-0eabb7389f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xz-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265148}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a355c26c-a760-5643-9305-ddb34ec96b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "062ec003-5729-5fe0-9819-864cef26e212" :question/math-academy-id "q-132100" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,i+(\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2})\\,j-2yz\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{y}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{y}-{z}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =-{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int (-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-y{z}^{2}+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=-y{z}^{2}+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2yz$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2yz & =-2yz+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+x-y{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "15b0b7b5-261c-5133-9040-81b3cde4ef13" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421424 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b6c4808-c2a7-5c97-8912-c4f3de3ef57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} {:answer/id #uuid "7c064342-1507-5fe3-91dc-0ff15726350b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421419} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe7055-048b-506e-b0e8-41cb887f2884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421420} {:answer/id #uuid "8d8717c4-00ef-5595-aeca-97e8db51eb3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y-x{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421421} {:answer/id #uuid "c86c75a8-56c7-544d-9bc9-6d9a38de128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+4y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421422}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421418} :answer-field/id #uuid "23f524e3-bd79-5da0-98c3-54cfe15b71e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49bf02f5-63a7-5afa-b61f-cb52dc9f8048" :question/math-academy-id "q-81738" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ where $F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+(4-2xy)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (3{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2xy+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-2xy$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2xy+{g}^{′}(y) & =4-2xy \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =4 \\\\ g(y) & =4y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=4y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{3}-x{y}^{2}+4y+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49bc2690-9ce7-5797-ab91-ca25964951a6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266133 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "005532ea-a569-5e6b-9518-a0150b3fb329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz{e}^{z}+x+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263006} {:answer/id #uuid "7d7694c1-e825-574d-a180-e04f18376fc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+x+{y}^{2}z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264082} {:answer/id #uuid "a49fbc3b-b203-5f1b-8f40-b0bcdbe9fbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{z}+y{x}^{2}+x-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264402} {:answer/id #uuid "c04be1f6-81c5-54c2-ba54-5b752c684e10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+x+y-z+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264673} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d7254b-5172-5dfd-9ea1-9404e617cc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186264739} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9c4e81f-33ab-54ad-9dac-693d04696388" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b7d90b3-0384-5231-a89f-b7683283ef28" :question/math-academy-id "q-132103" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ where $F(x,y,z)=(2x+y{e}^{z})\\,i+(x{e}^{z}+1)\\,j+(xy{e}^{z}-2z)\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x+y{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{z}+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{z}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =x{e}^{z}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=y+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+xy{e}^{z}+y+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy{e}^{z}-2z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy{e}^{z}-2z & =xy{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =-2z \\\\ h(z) & =-{z}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy{e}^{z}+{x}^{2}+y-{z}^{2}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "befa6245-a4cf-52d2-a6d8-3bd64bb32ca7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602f189-0d28-56ae-9dd9-e128132e6ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}+yz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256980} {:answer/id #uuid "56a824e1-89ad-5b9f-9d90-0a15c3c6afec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=xyz-y{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257529} {:answer/id #uuid "83844a24-d36a-5bde-90cc-25704c4bd024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} {:answer/id #uuid "9676b23d-58f9-5906-86c8-bc56f352ddbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+xz-{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258109} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfef173-d55d-5471-9bd3-2a9896819991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y,z)={y}^{3}z-x{e}^{z}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258987}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257944} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf730883-1f0b-511a-85de-b890c410b87c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "af3748dd-98e9-5ccb-8f93-b1501896f383" :question/math-academy-id "q-124733" :question/problem "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,i+2xy{z}^{3}\\,j+(3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z})\\,k.\n$$\n\nFind $f(x,y,z)$ such that $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{i}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,\\text{j}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\,\\text{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y,z)=F(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({y}^{2}{z}^{3}-{e}^{z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy{z}^{3}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xy{z}^{3}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =2xy{z}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we integrate $\\frac{∂g}{∂y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z) & =\\int \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\int 0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z}+{h}^{′}(z) & =3x{y}^{2}{z}^{2}-x{e}^{z} \\\\ {h}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ h(z) & =C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}-x{e}^{z}+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c075c9d7-1b99-540c-b91d-b2920031d6d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32cd3a46-2070-5c04-b850-8162eeb789a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348898} {:answer/id #uuid "b4d8f137-d9d9-5d7a-bf57-3e496c94821f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350047} {:answer/id #uuid "cd48e39f-f120-599e-9a6b-494a2b1af79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350251} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d34f2d-177c-57dd-a130-fa54336633eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} {:answer/id #uuid "ed148d5a-2003-5ebe-a806-ce910794f76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "f(x,y)=4xy+xy+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350468} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c564c77-be41-5cc9-9524-b2c24202e626" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3063cdc9-a985-5db1-8c3b-1970a3def78b" :question/math-academy-id "q-81734" :question/problem "Find $f(x,y)$ such that $∇f=F,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,i+(2{x}^{2}y+1)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the function $f(x,y)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,i+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nSince $∇f(x,y)=F(x,y),$ the function $f(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}+1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =\\int \\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2x{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+g(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2{x}^{2}y+1$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}y+{g}^{′}(y) & =2{x}^{2}y+1 \\\\ {g}^{′}(y) & =1 \\\\ g(y) & =y+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nFinally, substituting $g(y)=y+C$ into our expression for $f(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y+C.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "db028f1e-0fa4-5096-9db0-6954db49115e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421426}}] :activity/title "Calculating Potential Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421417}]}
